Disclaimer: I have not had the bandwidth to dive into the details of your design, but I noticed that in:

https://github.com/rstudio/promises/blob/25d7f854eadd47ad9187d3ece165ff91d5882cac/R/future_promise.R#L431-L459

it looks like you are playing the game of what `future::future()` does, i.e. capturing the current "state" (globals and packages), to be used later when the promise is resolved.

Have you considered using `future::future(..., lazy = TRUE)`? That will create the future, capturing the current state, without launching the future. Your call to `resolve(future_job)` should launch it.  I think you could simplify this to:

```r
future_promise <- function (..., envir = parent.frame(), queue = future_promise_queue()) {
    stopifnot(is.function(queue$schedule_work) && length(formals(queue$schedule_work)) >= 1)
    future_job <- future::future(..., envir = envir, lazy = TRUE)
    promise(function(resolve, reject) {
        queue$schedule_work(function() {
            resolve(future_job)
            future_job
        })
    })
}
```

This is a new issue with promises 1.3.1.

With long promise chains (such as those created by coro async_generator, including {elmer} chat objects' `stream_async()`) being executed inside of promise domains, we get ever-growing call stacks. This is the cause of the stack overflow seen in https://github.com/jcheng5/shinychat/issues/16.

Here is a minimal reprex; it prints out the number of calls on the call stack at each iteration of the async generator's for-loop. (We're using `shiny::withLogErrors The correct behavior is for the number to remain constant, but with promises 1.3.1, it grows.

```r
library(promises)
library(coro)

repro <- async(function() {
  for (i in 1:10) {
    await(async_sleep(0))
    message(length(sys.calls()))
  }
})

shiny::captureStackTraces({
  repro()
  while (!later::loop_empty()) {
    later::run_now()
  }
})
```

Note that this _only_ happens if promises are being _resolved_ while _inside_ a promise domain. The example below simply moves the `later` loop to outside of `shiny::captureStackTraces()`; the problem goes away.

```r
library(promises)
library(coro)

repro <- async(function() {
  for (i in 1:10) {
    await(async_sleep(0))
    message(length(sys.calls()))
  }
})

shiny::captureStackTraces({
  repro()
})
while (!later::loop_empty()) {
  later::run_now()
}
```

The example app below works for me locally but not on shinyapps.io. Specifically, if you press "Cancel," the task stops locally but not on shinyapps.io. Interestingly, it used to work, even with the same version of the package (in a larger app, see https://github.com/rstudio/rsconnect/issues/1105).

I know this is not the best way to handle aborting, but within the larger app and given the constraints I’m working with, it seems to be the only feasible option.

```
library(shiny)
future::plan(future::multisession)



# Define UI for application that draws a histogram
ui <- fluidPage(

  # Application title
  titlePanel("Cancelable Async Task"),

  # Sidebar with a slider input for number of bins
  sidebarLayout(
    sidebarPanel(
      actionButton("run", "Run"),
      actionButton("cancel", "Cancel")
    ),

    # Show a plot of the generated distribution
    mainPanel(
      tableOutput("result")
    )
  )
)

long_run_fun <- function(){
  start_time <- Sys.time()
  while (as.numeric(Sys.time() - start_time) < 10) {
    Sys.sleep(0.1)
    if (file.exists(abort_file)) {
      quit()
    }
  }
} 

server <- function(input, output) {
  fut <- NULL

  abort_file_global <- reactiveVal()
  running <- reactiveVal(FALSE)
  result_val <- reactiveVal()

  abort_file <- tempfile()
  abort_file_global(abort_file)
  showNotification("Starting Run")

  observeEvent(input$run, {
    # Don't do anything if in the middle of a run
    if (running()) {
      showNotification("Already Running")
      return(NULL)
    }
    running(TRUE)

    print("Starting Run")
    fut <- promises::future_promise({
      long_run_fun()
    }, globals = c("abort_file", "long_run_fun"))
    promises::then(
      fut,
      function(value) {
        print("Task Completed")
        showNotification("Task Completed")
      }
    )

    promises::catch(
      fut,
      function(e) {
        print("Task Aborted")
        showNotification("Task Aborted")
      }
    )

    promises::finally(fut, function() {
      running(FALSE)
      if (file.exists(abort_file)) {
        file.remove(abort_file)
      }
    })

    # Return something other than the future so we don't block the UI
    return(NULL)
  })


  # Kill future
  observeEvent(input$cancel, {
    abort_file_global()
    file.create(abort_file_global())
  })


  output$result <- renderTable({
    req(result_val())
  })
}

# Run the application
shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)
